export const lsConcepts301400: any[] = [
  { id: "ls-nama-301", title: "Hrīṅkārī", category: "Vāk-cit", summary: "She who is the form of syllable 'hrīṃ'. The name identifies Her with the great māyā seed-syllable, the Śākta praṇava matching the syllable Om in weight, also called the Bhuvaneśvarī seed. Its anatomy joins ha for outward manifestation, ra for the folding-inward power of illusion, ī for ripeness and perfection, ma with the bindu point that governs all three. Doctrinally the seed is read as the union of the still ground and its kinetic power: ha carries the Śiva spark, ī carries the Kāmakalā goddess-force, and ra welds them, while the bindu unfolds as source, seed and resonance from which creating, sustaining and dissolving arise. Sound itself is traced from the supreme unuttered level down through beheld, mental and spoken stages to embodied speech. Contemplate: hear each inward repetition of hrīṃ as the veil of seeming thinning over awareness.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-070"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-302", title: "Hrīmatī", category: "Vāk-cit", summary: "She who is endowed with modesty. Hrī names the blush of sacred reserve, and the tradition reads it as one face of Her veiling power, grouped with mind, contentment, longing and nourishment. The point is not prudery but selectivity: She will not enter rites performed for display, vanity or bargaining, feeling, as it were, shame before pomp. Hence Her worship is enjoined as inward and quiet rather than theatrical, an offering made inside attention rather than before an audience. Through such interior adoration the subtler figures of the Goddess, the wish-art form and the coiled serpent power, disclose themselves more swiftly than through elaborate externals. Contemplate: draw one daily practice behind closed eyes and offer it to Her in silence.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-070"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-303", title: "Hṛdyā", category: "Vāk-cit", summary: "She who abides in the heart. Hṛdyā says the heart is Her address: the chamber where the individual spark dwells, pictured by the seers as a thumb-sized flame-like presence at the body's center. Heart here also means tenderness, so the name confesses both Her residence and Her character as the compassionate Mother in whom love is structural rather than occasional. A further reading turns the grammar around: She is dear to every heart, loved spontaneously by all beings. Doctrinally the heart is where the scattered senses can be gathered and the Mother met without travel. Contemplate: rest attention on the heart-space and greet Her there before any outer prayer.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-070"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-304", title: "Heyopādeyavarjitā", category: "Vāk-cit", summary: "She who has nothing to reject or accept. Heya is what the rule-books bid us shun and upādeya what they bid us take up, and She stands free of both urgencies. Injunctions and prohibitions belong to the human order: ancestral custom codified into treatises, useful in their season but time-bound and revisable as conditions change. To follow them uncomprehendingly, or to mistake them for absolutes, misses their purpose, which is the training of embodied persons. The unconditioned ground neither gains by our permissions nor loses by our refusals. Contemplate: before judging any act prescribed or forbidden, ask what bondage it loosens here and now.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-070"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-305", title: "Rājarājārcitā", category: "Vāk-cit", summary: "She who is worshipped by the King of kings. Rājarāja first of all names Śiva Himself, so the verse pictures the supreme teacher adoring His own beloved, honoring motherhood in the very act of ruling the worlds. A second reckoning counts twelve royal votaries, beginning with the lords of wealth and primeval law, each honoring Her through his own recension of the fifteen-syllabled mantra whose seed-order never varies. Their title of kinghood is thus shown as conferred rather than seized: sovereignty flows from devotion to Her. Read with the following name, the image is of empire itself laying its crown at Her feet. Contemplate: offer whatever small authority you hold back to its source in Her.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-071"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-306", title: "Rājñī", category: "Vāk-cit", summary: "She who is the queen of shiva, the Lord of all kings. Rājñī is the natural counterpart of the preceding adoration: where He is saluted as sovereign over sovereigns, She takes Her seat beside Him as mistress of the same cosmos. The universe is thus shown as governed not by solitary command but by a wedded pair, stillness and power sharing one throne. Devotion shortens the royal distance by calling Her Mā, Mother, a word that replaces awe with kinship and makes the infinite feel addressed to oneself. The queen remains approachable precisely because She mothers what She rules. Contemplate: address Her once today simply as Mother and notice what softens.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-071"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-307", title: "Ramyā", category: "Vāk-cit", summary: "She who gives delight; She who is lovely. Ramyā compresses Her whole aesthetic theology into one epithet: She pleases and She is pleasing, the joy and its fountain together. After the royal names, beauty appears here not as ornament but as governance by attraction, the cosmos held less by edict than by charm. The tradition refuses to separate delight given to devotees from loveliness beheld in Her, since both flow from the same fullness. Short as the name is, it anchors the longer sequence on the senses: queenship made visible as radiance. Contemplate: let one beautiful thing today remind you of its giver.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-071"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-308", title: "Rājīvalocanā", category: "Vāk-cit", summary: "She whose eyes are like rājiva (lotus). Rājīva ranges across deer, fish and lotus, so the poets had many ready-made eyes to borrow, yet here they reach past formula toward the incomparable: eyes full of grace that create, sustain and dissolve with a wink. The lotus reading keeps Her gaze pure and unfolding, the deer and fish readings keep it swift, watchful and alive in its waters. A further play hears rāji as kingship, making Her the compassionate regard resting on all who depend on the great King. No single simile exhausts such eyes, which bless exactly by seeing. Contemplate: imagine those eyes resting a moment on your own face.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-071"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-309", title: "Rañjanī", category: "Vāk-cit", summary: "She who delights the mind. Rañjanī names the act of coloring, and everything about Her takes that warm dye: the Goddess glows red, and even the crystal-clear Lord beside Her blushes with reflected light when She sits near Him. The doctrine underneath the poetry is that joy is not added to reality but is its tint; to be near Her is to be saturated. Devotees receive gladness in this life and a gladness that outlasts it, since delight here rehearses freedom from return. The old hymn to Her beauty pictures the still Lord Himself turned rosy like a coverlet steeped in Her luster. Contemplate: picture that red glow settling over your thoughts until they quiet.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-071"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-310", title: "Ramaṇī", category: "Vāk-cit", summary: "She who gives joy. Ramaṇī shows the Mother at play, amusing Herself with Her children and amusing them in turn, since providing happiness and sporting with the happy belong together in motherhood. Devotees too often spoil the game by keeping a fearful distance, treating reverence as separation and imagining the all-present as someone else. The Upaniṣadic vision corrects this: the freed self rises into light and moves about eating, playing and delighting, unremembering the old body-stall, because Self and ground are one. Omnipresence means She shares your poverty and plenty, your onion and your feast. Contemplate: invite Her into one ordinary pleasure today instead of apart from it.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-071"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-311", title: "Rasyā", category: "Vāk-cit", summary: "She who is to be enjoyed; She who enjoys. Rasyā places Her where the ancient identification of the Real with savor puts it: the Real is sweetness itself, and whoever holds that sweetness is happy. The name works in both directions at once, naming Her the supreme relish sought by knowers and the enjoyer in whom all tasting gathers. Since the source of savor is the Self, bliss is reached not by piling up objects but by recognizing the taster. She is that condensed totality in which cosmos and inward witness coincide. Contemplate: taste one sip or breath as though sweetness itself were greeting you.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-071"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-312", title: "Raṇatkiṅkiṇimekhalā", category: "Vāk-cit", summary: "She who wears a girdle of tinkling bells. Mekhalā is first of all adornment, the golden waist-band with tiny bells familiar from the hymns to Her slender waist, but the commentators hear metaphysics in the music: the tinkle marks the birthplace of audible sound at the navel wheel. Advance is therefore mapped through four modes of Her figure, gross for beginners to picture, mantra-like for the subtle, wish-art for the subtler, and serpent-power for the subtlest, the earlier descriptive names supplying the first of these. Ornament thus doubles as pedagogy, beauty teaching the ear where speech is born. Contemplate: listen to any small outer sound as rising from that inner navel bell.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-071"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-313", title: "Ramā", category: "Vāk-cit", summary: "She who has become lakṣmī and sarasvatī. Ramā gathers both goddesses of fortune and learning into one person, promising prosperity that is at once material sufficiency and spiritual wealth. The name also serves the esoteric alphabet: together with the next two names it builds the seed-syllable of the wish-art, supplying its opening vowel Ī. Devotion that seeks Her under this name therefore asks for abundance in both registers without dividing them. Fortune and insight appear as two faces of a single gracious turning toward the devotee. Contemplate: ask Her today for one outer need and one inner clarity in the same breath.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-072"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-314", title: "Rākenduvadanā", category: "Vāk-cit", summary: "She who has a delightful face like the full moon. Rākendu is the spotless full orb, and the comparison is exact rather than decorative: the moon-disc figures the point, the bindu, set above the vowel of the previous name, ripening Ī into the seed-syllable Īṃ. What was an open sound now carries its dot of concentration, on the verge of becoming the wish-art itself. The untarnished moon also tells Her character, favor that waxes toward fullness rather than waning. A face like that announces that a formative power is about to round itself whole. Contemplate: picture a full moon rising over the sound Ī until it seals into a single glowing drop.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-072"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-315", title: "Ratirūpā", category: "Vāk-cit", summary: "She who is in the form of rati, the wife of kāma. Rati is the consort of the love-god, and the name delivers what the last two prepared: the seed-syllable now stands complete and quickens into the wish-art, the Goddess as the lovely power of attraction. Desire here is not condemned but transfigured, since the same current that binds, rightly turned, creates and hallows. The auspicious undercurrent beneath erotic play is the will toward manifestation itself, creation wearing the mask of longing. Thus a name that sounds amorous carries a cosmogonic weight. Contemplate: offer the energy of attraction itself at Her feet and watch it turn creative.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-072"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-316", title: "Ratipriyā", category: "Vāk-cit", summary: "She who is fond of rati; She who is served by rati. Ratipriyā turns from archetype to retinue: She cherishes the love-goddess and is attended by her, delighting in the very power of delight. Popular lore knows a wealth-yakṣiṇī of the same title, counted as the treasurer-god's wife, whose short mantra is said to be rehearsed by night-seekers through a hundred thousand rounds with closing rites. Whether read as cosmic affection or as the folk bestower of riches, the pattern holds: love served returns as plenty. The name binds eros to abundance without apology. Contemplate: serve one loving impulse well and observe what prospers around it.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-072"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-317", title: "Rakṣākarī", category: "Vāk-cit", summary: "She who is the protector. Rakṣākarī states Her sustaining office plainly: the universe persists because She guards it. Ritual imagination gives the image a second edge in the ashes of the sacred fire, where burnt herbs are sealed into amulets against harm; ash that shields the living also recalls the mortal remains of the dead, so protection and dissolution meet in one emblem. Of the ground's three doings, guarding and withdrawing are both hinted here, with fuller sacrificial detail reserved for later names. Shelter and ending belong to the same hand. Contemplate: entrust one vulnerable part of your life to that guarding hand tonight.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-072"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-318", title: "Rākṣasaghnī", category: "Vāk-cit", summary: "She who is the slayer of the entire race of demons. Rākṣasaghnī answers the promise of the Gītā that the Real appears age after age to shield the good, ruin the ruinous, and reset the right. Demons here are read inwardly as entrenched evils rather than merely mythic armies, the forces that rot persons and ages from within. When corruption becomes total, the lore foresees dissolution clearing the ground for a fresh creation. Destruction is therefore shown as surgery on history, the precondition of restored order.  Her appearances punctuate history whenever falsehood grows bold, restoring the teachers, the rites and the inward disciplines together. Contemplate: name one inner tyrant plainly and ask Her warrior-side to meet it.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-072"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-319", title: "Rāmā", category: "Vāk-cit", summary: "She who gives delight. Rāmā first declares Her the very pattern of womanhood: all men the still Lord, all women the power, says the old Purāṇa, whence the warning that dishonored women blight a lineage. Ram also names delight, and the fire-seed it spells is prized for kindling whatever syllables it joins, carrying them toward blessedness. Yogins know the reference, for they call the rejoicing of the united pair at the crown by this same gladness, the delight in which knowers dissolve. The name thus joins ethics, mantra craft and mystic joy. Contemplate: honor the feminine in one concrete act of respect today.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-072"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-320", title: "Ramaṇalampaṭā", category: "Vāk-cit", summary: "She who is devoted to the Lord of Her heart, Lord shiva. Ramaṇalampaṭā turns delight into fidelity, picturing Her absorbed in every moment with Her consort at the thousand-petaled crown, tasting wedded bliss at the summit of the body. Play ripens into constancy, and constancy radiates outward: as the pattern of womanhood from the previous name, She shapes wives toward devotion to their husbands. Union at the height becomes the template for union in the home. The erotic and the domestic mirror one another without embarrassment.  The summit-wedding thus hallows ordinary fidelity, teaching that tenderness rightly ordered climbs from household affection to crown-union. Contemplate: carry one moment of quiet tenderness into your nearest bond today.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-072"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-321", title: "Kāmyā", category: "Vāk-cit", summary: "She who is to be desired. Kāmyā reverses the gaze: She whom all desiring seeks is Herself the proper object of longing, craved above all by those who thirst for release. Liberation comes only through knowing, and She is that knowing in person, so desire for freedom correctly terminates in Her. The lunar calendar keeps the memory, naming the twelfth night of the dark fortnight after longing. Wanting rightly is therefore the first rung of the ladder She provides.  Right longing therefore outranks all other longings, since every lesser want borrows its sweetness from this single deserving object. Contemplate: test one desire by asking whether it leads nearer to Her or farther away.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-073"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-322", title: "Kāmakalārūpā", category: "Vāk-cit", summary: "She who is in the form of kāmakalā. Kāmakalā is Her subtler figure, known in full only to Her Spouse, standing between mantra-form and the serpent-power at the crown. Kāma here means the supremely desirable, the still Lord as highest reality and ruler, while kalā means His reflective part, the Goddess as His self-awareness; their conjunction is the divine pair in creative poise, fairest as world-mother, world-father. Three seed-points above and an inverted triangle below figure sun, moon and fire, nourishing breasts and commanding brow, and the three mantra-families from which all sacred utterances branch. The old hymn bids meditation on face, bosom and half-letter as one diagram. Contemplate: approach this mystery only with reverence, preferably under a trusted guide.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-073"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-323", title: "Kadambakusumapriyā", category: "Vāk-cit", summary: "She who is especially fond of kadamba flowers. Kadamba blossoms ring Her dwelling in the grove where She lives, and their scent attends Her like a signature. The name also recurs early in the three-hundred litany, whose number is woven from the fifteen mantra-seeds times twenty, each score of names opening with successive seed-letters. Lore counts the kadamba among five holy trees answering to mind, intellect, awareness, ego and the heart where the spark resides, its perfume answering the drift and change of mental states. Botany becomes psychology in Her garden. Contemplate: breathe in one flower's fragrance as an emblem of the mind She steadies.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-073"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-324", title: "Kalyāṇī", category: "Vāk-cit", summary: "She who bestows auspiciousness. Kalyāṇī is nobility, generosity and worth made personal, the power by which occasions turn favorable and persons turn worthy. The ancient Veda already uses the word for what is deserving, and the three-hundred litany places it near its very threshold. Auspiciousness here is not luck but a transmissible energy, felt as uplift in Her presence and capable of being caught like a resonance. To praise Her thus is to claim that goodness itself has an address.  Such favor shows in steadied undertakings, cleared obstacles and a quieter mind, the outward signature of an inward benediction. Contemplate: begin one undertaking by invoking Her worthiness and note the steadier tone it lends.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-073"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-325", title: "Jagatīkandā", category: "Vāk-cit", summary: "She who is the root of the whole world. Jagatīkanda drives the search for origins past every branch to the bulb underground: the world-tree grows from Her. The ground's stature is repeatedly pressed in this hymn through many attributes, and here it is stated botanically rather than argued. As the union of self-shining light and its reflective awareness, the great veiling power, She puts forth the cosmos while remaining its hidden tuber. Effects diversify while the cause abides unseen.  Philosophies may dispute names for the first principle, yet all confess some hidden ground sustaining the branching many. Contemplate: trace one familiar thing backward toward its root until thought touches soil it cannot see.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-073"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-326", title: "Karuṇārasasāgarā", category: "Vāk-cit", summary: "She who is the ocean of compassion. Karuṇārasasāgarā makes pity not a mood but an element, vast, salty and inexhaustible, natural to Her as mother of all. The three-hundred litany sings the same tide as an ocean of mercy's nectar. The great teacher's image clarifies the paradox: the sea never moves, yet its exhaled drop crosses heaven and earth to fall as rain that feeds the fields, gaining nothing for itself. So Her grace descends impartially on all, distinguishing no favorites while sustaining every life.  No worthiness qualifies the recipient beforehand, for a mother does not audit her children before nursing them through the night. Contemplate: receive one small undeserved kindness today as a drop from that ocean.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-073"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-327", title: "Kalāvatī", category: "Vāk-cit", summary: "She who is the embodiment of all arts. Kalāvatī holds the sixty-four crafts of sacred science, already surveyed at an earlier name and rehearsed here with a fine distinction: there She was their very form, here their possessor, shining as object and reflection together. The arts are traced to the eight great attainments and declared by the Lord at His consort's prompting; the hymn to Her beauty says all sixty-four arise from the fifteenfold mantra and return into it. Since She is not different from that mantra, to know Her is to hold the sum of techniques. Contemplate: dedicate one skill you practice, however humble, as one of Her sixty-four.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-074"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-328", title: "Kalālāpā", category: "Vāk-cit", summary: "She who speaks musically and sweetly. Kalālāpā hears art in Her very utterance: kalā as melody and ālāpa as address meet in a voice that instructs by delighting. The shorter litany repeats the epithet, and the commentators derive whole curricula from that sweetness, whether the sixteen mantra-seeds with Goddess and guide completing eighteen, or the eighteen branches of learning from phonetics to statecraft. From Her conversation the sciences mature, giving the mind power to sift fair from foul. Speech becomes schooling.  Whoever drinks deeply of that utterance learns discernment, since true learning sharpens judgment about conduct and consequence. Contemplate: speak one sentence today slowly and sweetly enough to teach.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-074"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-329", title: "Kāntā", category: "Vāk-cit", summary: "She who is beautiful. Kāntā follows the music with radiance: earlier the auspicious throbbed, now loveliness visibly vibrates and shines from Her gross figure. Yet the syllables carry a second freight, for ka names the ground itself and anta the end, making the beautiful One also the final term of all seeking, the ultimate. Form and formless meet in a single epithet, delight for the eye and destination for the mind. Beauty is thus both veil and signpost.  Worshippers therefore approach loveliness itself as a door, entering through delight toward the changeless delight that never fades. Contemplate: let one lovely sight lead your thought past itself to its source.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-074"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-330", title: "Kādambarīpriyā", category: "Vāk-cit", summary: "She who is fond of mead. Kādambarī is the honeyed ferment gathered with kadamba bloom, one of the five charged substances honored in the ritual of the nine enclosures with its special cup. Such left-hand worship, with its wine, flesh, fish, union and gesture, demands a masterful guide and is not commended for casual practice. Read inwardly, the draught is the nectar released when the coiled power reaches the crown, or again the devotion of true lovers by which the Goddess Herself seems delightfully overtaken. Other draught-names in this hymn vary the emblem with context. Contemplate: offer Her the intoxication of sincere attention rather than any outer stimulant.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-074"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-331", title: "Varadā", category: "Vāk-cit", summary: "She who grants boons generously. Varadā is the common office of all bright powers, often signed by the raised palm, yet Her manner is distinctive: it is Her holy feet, not Her hands, that confer, as the hymn to Her beauty insists, sheltering the fearful and outgiving every request. The earlier names of wish-fulfilment and devotee-prosperity converge here and confirm one another. Tradition adds that meditation on the ninth lunar day finds Her especially free-handed toward all worlds. Grace, in short, walks on feet before it lifts a hand. Contemplate: lay one request mentally at Her feet rather than grasping it in your hands.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-074"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-332", title: "Vāmanayanā", category: "Vāk-cit", summary: "She who has beautiful eyes. Vāmanayanā is read two ways at once: lovely eyes, and the guiding of action's fruits toward Her, since vāma also means the harvest of deeds and leading means liberation. The Upaniṣad of chant confirms the pun, calling the knower of Self the concentration of all fair things, who conveys every good to beings according to desert, himself the stay of purity. Fairness of feature thus opens into fairness of judgment, beauty becoming providence. To be seen by those eyes is to be assigned one's rightful good. Contemplate: trust one outcome to Her apportioning instead of forcing it.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-074"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-333", title: "Vāruṇī madavivhalā", category: "Vāk-cit", summary: "She who is intoxicated by vāruṇi (ambrosial drink). Vāruṇī is date-liquor left to brew, and the picture shows Her deliciously forgetful of surroundings, gathered into Her inner Self, Her Lord. A bodily parallel is claimed in the subtle channel of the same name, governing release, which breath-discipline masters so that sages endure long absorption without fatigue; She is said to ensoul that channel. That such fine anatomy hides inside a drinking-image proves, say the transmitters, more than human contrivance. Fuller draught-lore waits for a later name.  Sober practice imitates that rapture by gathering attention inward until outer noise dissolves into single-pointed absorption. Contemplate: practice one slow breath-cycle as a sober sip of inwardness.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-074"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-334", title: "Viśvādhikā", category: "Vāk-cit", summary: "She who transcends the universe. Viśvādhikā sets Her past every constituent the schools enumerate, the thirty-six principles from the still source down through veiling powers, inner instruments, senses and the five great elements to earth. Living beings exist by these principles, yet She who wields them is not compounded of them. Transcendence here is not distance but priority: the weaver exceeds the web. To cross the all is to stand where counting stops.  Scriptures count principles only to surpass them, using enumeration as a ladder kicked away once the summit is seen. Contemplate: remind yourself that whatever you can list is not yet Her.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-075"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-335", title: "Vedavedyā", category: "Vāk-cit", summary: "She who is known through the vedas. Vedavedyā confesses that all sacred lore terminates in the Real, which the Gītā-voice claims as its own object and knower together, to be met by all the Vedas. Mere literacy will not do: the lore must ripen into wisdom through lived practice of knowing, doing and adoring, else the verses stay pretty sounds about outer fires while their inward sense sleeps. The four gates of Her mystic diagram answer the four Vedas, and perfect knowledge is distinguished from mere ritual science. The hymn calls Her the very sap of those texts. Contemplate: read one verse less for information than for transformation.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-075"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-336", title: "Vindhyācalanivāsinī", category: "Vāk-cit", summary: "She who resides in the vindhya mountains. Vindhyavāsinī recalls the seven-hundred's promise that She would be born in the cowherd's house and dwell in these hills to destroy the two great demons of arrogance. The clue quietly identifies the cowherd prince with the Mother Herself, explaining why this thousand so often wears the maintainer's colors. Kinship language elsewhere calls Her His sister under His lotus-navel title, binding the Vaiṣṇava and Śākta visions. Geography becomes theology: a mountain range remembers an incarnation.  Pilgrims still climb those slopes seeking audience, trusting that stone, forest and shrine remember what books can only narrate. Contemplate: honor wild and marginal places as Her chosen camps.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-075"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-337", title: "Vidhātrī", category: "Vāk-cit", summary: "She who creates and sustains this unverse. Vidhātrī joins mothering to making: dhātrī is the nurse, and the supreme Mother nurses the worlds by feeding them into being and keeping them fed. The word also names the gooseberry sacred to fortune's goddess, which She is said to favor. Under creation's three offices the one ground appears as maker, keeper and withdrawer with matching consorts of learning, plenty and transformative power; later names will ring changes on the same offices without ever merely repeating. Sameness of function never means sameness of sense. Contemplate: nourish one person or project today in Her nurse-like manner.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-075"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-338", title: "Vedajananī", category: "Vāk-cit", summary: "She who is the mother of the vedas. Vedajananī says the Vedas were born of Her, breaking from the ground as sound itself, caught by ancient seers in high attunement and handed down mouth to ear, writing arriving only late. Hence the anxious care for accent and tone, since a slipped note misdirects the force; hence too the classifications of lower lore versus the higher knowing of the imperishable, and the vision of scriptures as the very breathing of the infinite. One psalm of the cosmic Person traces them to the gods' primal fire-offering. All accounts agree the Word exhales from the Supreme. Contemplate: chant or hear one syllable attentively as Her newborn child.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-075"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-339", title: "Viṣṇumāyā", category: "Vāk-cit", summary: "She who is the illusory power of viṣṇu. Viṣṇumāyā places Her as the veiling brightness of the all-pervading keeper, the maintainer's own enigma. Stillness grants Her full autonomy to administer the worlds, and that autonomy shows as the shimmer that hides the Real while displaying it. The Gītā-voice warns that this glamour of the three strands is hard to cross, then gives the pass: those who hand themselves over pass beyond it. To manipulate the strands is to manifest; to surrender is to wake. Worship of power is therefore the keeper's own prescribed door. Contemplate: surrender one controlling effort and watch the veil thin.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-075"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-340", title: "Vilāsinī", category: "Vāk-cit", summary: "She who is playful. Vilāsinī lets the keeper's glamour dance: dalliance with Her Lord, far from fault, keeps creation's channel open, and the old books hedge such union with careful observance rather than refusal. Beneath the dalliance lies projection-power, the capacity to hide the true and show it otherwise, which is illusion's very engine. Coming directly after the name of the maintainer's veil, the play explains how the veil moves. The world is the God's game seriously played.  To call the cosmos play dignifies enjoyment without licensing excess, since games too have rules and stakes worth honoring. Contemplate: meet one amusement without guilt, seeing Her mischief in it.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-075"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-341", title: "Kṣetrasvarūpā", category: "Vāk-cit", summary: "She whose body is matter. Kṣetrasvarūpā opens the field-sequence by recalling that the body is the field where deeds are sown and reaped, compounded of the principles the schools count as thirty-six or twenty-four. The Gītā's field-chapter calls the frame the field and its knower the field-knower, while the old Purāṇa assigns the Goddess as field to the Lord's knower. Field is gross and perishing, knower subtle and undying, and those who grasp the difference with release from nature's evolutes reach the eternal spirit. She wears all gross shapes as Her own limbs. Contemplate: view your body as Her acre, to be tilled rather than owned.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-076"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-342", title: "Kṣetreśī", category: "Vāk-cit", summary: "She who is the wife of kṣetreśa (shiva). Kṣetreśī pairs the knower with His knowing bride as the awe-inspiring pair are paired, reminding that stillness and power never divide. The title may equally be heard as mistress of all fields, extending the previous name from one body to every embodied place. Either way the bridal image guards non-duality: two names, one reality. The field finds its lord, the lord his field.  Temple and body mirror one another, each a field awaiting its knower, each knower awaiting reunion with its field. Contemplate: greet the knower in yourself as wedded, never widowed, to its field.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-076"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-343", title: "Kṣetrakṣetrajñapālinī", category: "Vāk-cit", summary: "She who is the protector of matter and the knower of matter, therefore the protector of body and soul. Kṣetrakṣetrajñapālinī guards both terms at once, the perishing frame and its undying witness, lest either be imagined outside Her care. Guarding the witness means guarding the Lord Himself as His wife must, while guarding the frame means mothering every child of clay as the universal Mother must; the poet's salutation of the world's father and mother belongs here. Heaven's pair models earth's parentage.  Such double guardianship leaves nothing orphaned, neither the flesh that labors nor the spark that watches within it. Contemplate: commend both your health and your awareness to one keeping.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-076"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-344", title: "Kṣayavṛddhivinirmuktā", category: "Vāk-cit", summary: "She who is free from growth and decay. Kṣayavṛddhivinirmuktā crowns the field-arc with the ground's own immunity: having been shown as body, bride and keeper, She is now shown as beyond the body's arithmetic. Mortals swell and shrink, but the witness neither rises with good work nor sinks with bad, as the forest-teaching says, and the Gītā-voice calls the self unborn, enduring and free of waning. Function never stains essence.  Ages may calcine mountains into dust while that essence abides unspent, the silent measure against which all change is weighed. Contemplate: rest in what in you neither improves nor deteriorates.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-076"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-345", title: "Kṣetrapālasamarcitā", category: "Vāk-cit", summary: "She who is worshipped by kṣetrapāla (shiva in infant form). Kṣetrapāla is the field's guard, first of all the elemental wardens of earth, water, fire, air and space who adore Her. Then comes the wonder-tale: the dark warrior-goddess, unappeased after slaying the demon of obstruction, was met by stillness in a baby's form, and nursing Him drank in Her own fury, saving the worlds; the babe-guard was adored by Her and adores Her still. The sacrificial ground itself is also called field, with its own keeper at the gate. Contemplate: honor the guardians of your own ground, outer and inner.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-076"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-346", title: "Vijayā", category: "Vāk-cit", summary: "She who is ever-victorious. Vijayā never loses, whether routing the demon hosts or, more tenderly, conquering the beloved's heart. Mastery of arms images mastery of sense, since victories abroad rehearse the inner conquest without which the Goddess stays unseen. The calendar keeps Her triumph on the tenth autumn evening, whose twilight is prized for beginnings, and the mystic diagram honors a lunar attendant of the same name. Winning is Her habit because wholeness cannot be defeated.  Festivals still borrow Her title for their climactic day, trusting that ventures launched under victory ripen toward success. Contemplate: begin one hard thing in Her victorious hour and lend it Her name.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-077"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-347", title: "Vimalā", category: "Vāk-cit", summary: "She who is without a trace of impurity. Vimalā denies every stain, where stain means the ignorance-born sense of lack that films the soul and blocks the free shining of the Self. The prefix reverses the word as in the earlier stainless name, and the two together distinguish mind-born from matter-born defilement, both belonging to power's own stuff. Such film hardens into ego, the grain-of-selfhood disease, breeding darkness, confusion and unknowing. Release comes by loosening clutch on things and dissolving the clutcher, until knowing dawns.  Meditation on stainlessness gradually bleaches the film, thought by thought, until the mirror of awareness reflects clearly again. Contemplate: wipe one small possessiveness clean as an offering to the Stainless.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-077"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-348", title: "Vandyā", category: "Vāk-cit", summary: "She who is adorable, worthy of worship. Vandyā says hearts bow to Her untaught, drawn by the divine charge She radiates through the frame like warmth through cloth. The lore traces the current entering by the crown-mouth and the hollow at the skull's base, passing the inward eye and the pineal light along one straight road, and issuing behind the head to cleanse the subtle envelope. Such ripening both hastens the spirit and sometimes leaves bright capacities in its wake. She draws from the supreme stillness above Her and pours it through the worlds. Contemplate: sit upright and imagine that quiet current descending through you.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-077"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-349", title: "Vandārujanavatsalā", category: "Vāk-cit", summary: "She who is full of motherlly love for those who worship Her. Vandārujanavatsalā extends adoration into affection: as the cow turns wholly toward her calf, so the Mother melts toward whoever turns toward Her. Love, like scent from a flower, steams from the body unbidden and is read by others without words; even a fed dog answers the giver's ray with a wagging tail. Tenderness is thus a physics of vibration before it is a sentiment. To be loved by Her is to be steeped in that field.  Such affection answers prayer faster than argument, for love travels where reasoning only points from afar. Contemplate: feed one creature today and feel the answering circuit close.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-077"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-350", title: "Vāgvādinī", category: "Vāk-cit", summary: "She who speaks. Vāgvādinī prompts all utterance and is utterance itself, the source from which the speech-goddess receives her portfolio as ministers divide offices in a court. Since the Word's origin is the Mother, every true sentence borrows Her breath. Sarasvatī's lute is strung by an older hand. To praise speech in Her is to confess that articulation is grace before it is skill.  Every alphabet therefore prays before it speaks, and every poet owes Her royalties on each well-turned line.  Grammarians call speech Her echo, and silence Her home, honoring both utterance and its luminous source. Contemplate: before your next important words, silently ask Her to tune them.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-077"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-351", title: "Vāmakeśī", category: "Vāk-cit", summary: "She who has beautiful hair. Vāmakeśī is heard first as the bride of the lord of the left-hand revelation, the twenty-eightfold tantra corpus containing one scripture devoted wholly to Her cult. Vāmaka means mortal man, so its lord is the chief among men, and His wife takes His name as awe-inspiring pairs do. The syllables also simply sing lovely tresses, though the neighboring names of learning and fire-circles point toward fortune's goddess enthroned between them. A later name of nearly the same sound will name the sixty-fifth scripture, supreme manual of inward adoration, where even the great seed is spelled in cipher of gods. Contemplate: gather your scattered rites into one inward offering to the fair-haired Mother.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-077"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-352", title: "Vahnimaṇḍalavāsinī", category: "Vāk-cit", summary: "She who resides in the disc of fire. Vahnimaṇḍala is the sphere of flame, located by some teachers at the root wheel where She already dwells, and by others in the ether where fire also secretly pervades. Flame further spells the number three, picturing moon at the crown, sun at the heart and fire at the root strung along the spine, all of which She ensouls. The fifteenfold mantra likewise stands in three families, perhaps why it is counted supreme. Residence thus means identity: where radiance concentrates, there She sits. Contemplate: kindle attention at the body's base and feel warmth rise as Her presence.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-077"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-353", title: "Bhaktimatkalpalatikā", category: "Vāk-cit", summary: "She who is the kalpa (wish-granting) creeper to Her devotees. Kalpa is the heaven-tree beneath which sitters obtain their wish, and She spreads over the earth after that manner, covering multitudes like a vine. Imperfection is also read in the word, so that faltering devotion is not refused but ripened across lives until it turns perfect and wins release without return. Mere sojourn in bright worlds differs from this final freedom, since heavens exhaust their merit while Her grace ends birth itself. The Gītā-voice agrees that only striving seers perceive the indwelling presence. Contemplate: sit mentally beneath Her branches and frame one wish worth keeping.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-078"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-354", title: "Paśupāśavimocinī", category: "Vāk-cit", summary: "She who releases the ignorant from bondage. Paśu is the beast that knows only fodder, here the soul that knows only externals, distinct from the universal soul yet imagining itself cut off. Its rope is unknowing, and the forest Upaniṣad promises that whoever wakes to the ground as Self becomes the all, as gods and seers before have done. The old aphorism calls vitiated knowing itself the fetter, an inborn film over being and over intellect that drags souls through birth and death. The Lord of herds as master and She as Mother together cut that cord for devotees. Contemplate: confess one unknowing you carry and ask to be unbound from it.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-078"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-355", title: "Saṃhṛtāśeṣapāṣaṇḍā", category: "Vāk-cit", summary: "She who destroys all heretics. Pāṣaṇḍa names those who step outside the fourteen seats of right order, the Vedas with their limbs and branches, scorning the boundaries that keep life sacred. She who mothers the worlds must also police them, dissolving what poisons the common dharma. The severity belongs to surgery rather than spite: false teaching starves souls, so its removal is itself nourishment. Tolerance of persons never meant indifference to truth.  Upholding the banks of sacred order protects seekers upstream, lest polluted headwaters sicken every pilgrim below. Contemplate: examine whether your own novelties honor or erode the ancient banks.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-078"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-356", title: "Sadācārapravartikā", category: "Vāk-cit", summary: "She who is immersed in (and inspires others to follow) right conduct. Sadācāra joins the good with their track, the noble deeds of noble lives preserved in epic and law-book. Having cleared the previous name's obstruction, She now draws the unknowing, those still caught in twoness, onto that track toward the ground. Ignorance here is not want of schooling but want of unity, and conduct becomes the bridge. Righteous habit steadies the mind until it can bear non-duality.  Step by step the track climbs from courtesy toward contemplation, until good habits ripen into liberating insight. Contemplate: adopt one small noble practice and walk it daily toward Her.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-078"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-357", title: "Tāpatrayāgnisantaptasamāhlādanacandrikā", category: "Vāk-cit", summary: "She who is the moonlight that gives joy to those burned by the triple fire of misery. Three flames scorch the bound: inward disarray of mind, senses and organs; outward pressure of elements and embodiment; and overshadowing by powers beyond the human. Against all three She rises like cool moonbeams on fevered skin, Her mere grace quenching what effort cannot. The forest-teaching glimpses the unborn, undecaying, fearless Self that such cooling reveals. Misery is triple, but mercy is single and sufficient.  Devotees compare Her to sandal-paste on burns, cooling precisely where the smart is sharpest and relief seems impossible. Contemplate: when afflicted, turn your face upward to that inward moon.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-079"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-358", title: "Taruṇī", category: "Vāk-cit", summary: "She who is ever young. Taruṇī never ages because She never alters, and agelessness belongs only to the ground, which the forest-teaching calls undecaying and deathless. Earlier names of eternity, fullness and freedom from waxing and waning converge on this freshness. Youth here is not a stage of life but the changeless bloom of being itself. What cannot modify cannot wither.  Sages greet that freshness as proof of the imperishable, the one bloom that cosmetics can neither add nor remove.  Worshippers greet each dawn as Her mirror, proof that time polishes nothing belonging to eternity. Contemplate: greet the unfading in yourself beneath every tired feeling.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-079"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-359", title: "Tāpasārādhyā", category: "Vāk-cit", summary: "She who is worshipped by ascetics. Tāpasa are those who have renounced sweets to seek the ultimate alone, scorning lesser godlings for the Real; that such single-aimed ones adore Her proves Her the absolute they seek. A second hearing divides the word into bondage and the essence of deep absorption, teaching that worldly entanglement dissolves only in profound meditation on Her. Renunciation and contemplation thus meet in one epithet. The austere confirm what lovers confess.  Their salute carries special weight, since those who want nothing are hardest to impress and surest to recognize worth. Contemplate: renounce one small pleasure today and offer its space to Her.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-079"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-360", title: "Tanumadhyā", category: "Vāk-cit", summary: "She who is slender-waisted. Tanumadhyā praises the narrow middle already sung among Her graces, while also naming a classical meter that counts its syllables after Her measure. Meter is the body of verse as waist is the body of the Goddess, and She is said to wear such patterns as Her form. The Gītā-voice likewise claims the queen of meters as His own splendor. Prosody becomes physiology, poetry a girth.  Devotees who pace their breathing or chanting to such measure find the body itself turning into an orderly verse. Contemplate: set one prayer to a steady rhythm and feel Her measure in it.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-079"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-361", title: "Tamo'apahā", category: "Vāk-cit", summary: "She who removes the ignorance born of tamas. Tamas is the heavy strand, mental darkness, delusion and error, incarnate in the sluggish knower. The white-island teaching warns that mechanical ritualists stumble blind, while mere notion-worshippers of God sink deeper, since worship aimed at gain or show never ripens. Such mercenary piety is ignorance wearing devotion's mask. She lifts that specific gloom, clearing the eye rather than merely feeding the wish.  Offered motives draw Her answering clarity, while bargaining prayers return unheard like seeds sown upon stone.  Such clearing is itself Her gift, since the lamp of discernment is lit by the very hand it reveals. Contemplate: purify one motive today until no bargain remains in it.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-079"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-362", title: "Citiḥ", category: "Vāk-cit", summary: "She who is in the form of pure intelligence. Citi is knowing unmixed with unknowing, the vimarśa or reflective face of the Real set beside its self-shining stillness, each helpless without the other to keep the worlds. From their wedding all administration flows. The schools picture foundational awareness reflected in cosmic unknowing as God, and in separate unknowings as souls, moon-like lights borrowed from one sun. Being, awareness and bliss name that single essence thrice.  To abide knowingly in that knowing is already worship, since attention resting on awareness closes the gap between seeker and sought. Contemplate: notice the bare knowing in which these very words appear.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-080"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-363", title: "Tatpadalakṣyārthā", category: "Vāk-cit", summary: "She who is the embodiment of truth (which is indicated by the word 'tat'). Tatpada is the word That, the Real indicated obliquely in the great sentence identifying thou with That, confessing omnipresence and non-twoness. After the previous name's two faces of the ground, this one secures their identity as undifferentiated awareness, averting any split between still light and reflecting power. Lest shining-with-attributes seem other than shining-without, She is named the indirectly meant, the nirguṇa truth shining through all qualities.  Meditators hold the two words together until distinction softens, and the indicated truth stands forth without pointing. Contemplate: repeat inwardly thou art That until the two words lean together.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-080"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-364", title: "Cidekarasarūpiṇī", category: "Vāk-cit", summary: "She who is of the nature of the pure intelligence. She who is the cause of knowledge. Cidekarasa declares awareness one unbroken savor, refusing any gap between the conditioned knower and the unconditioned ground, since stewardship of worlds never stains the purity that wills it. Gross knowing and subtle knowing share one foundation as milk shares itself with all its products. She is therefore not different from awareness or from That of the preceding names, but their very pith extracted. Difference of office never divides essence.  No office, however lofty, dilutes that homogeneity, since waves never cease to be water while dancing as waves. Contemplate: sip experience to its essence until only knowing remains.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-080"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-365", title: "Svātmānandalavībhūtabrahmādyānandasantatiḥ", category: "Vāk-cit", summary: "She who makes the bliss of brahmā and others insignificant compared to Her own bliss. All the bright administrators taste joy, yet their summed delight is a single droplet beside Her sea, for every office from making to raining borrows its gladness from Her fullness. Our native state is that same happiness, only disturbed by grasping after what we lack and grieving what we lose. The lore of bliss pictures a gifted youth owning the world's wealth, then multiplies his peak contentment toward infinity to hint the ground's savor, reached by ascending through breath, mind, discernment and rapture into the cosmic Self. Contemplate: rest one minute in want-free awareness and taste its edge.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-080"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-366", title: "Parā", category: "Vāk-cit", summary: "She who is the supreme; She who transcends all. Parā opens the doctrine of sacred sound by naming its supreme, unuttered poise, the absolute as seed before sprouting. Still light and its realization stand wedded like word and sense, and from their union speech germinates through beholding, mental and uttered stages, as seed to sprout to sapling to tree. In the body the seed stirs at the root as coiled power, climbs to the navel beholding, the heart pondering, the throat utterance. Creation's own logic follows the story of twenty-five principles, where soul meets nature, veiling proportioned to past momentum, and the causal point quivers toward effect. Contemplate: listen behind your next sentence for the silence it grew from.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-081"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-367", title: "Pratyakcitīrūpā", category: "Vāk-cit", summary: "She who is of the nature of unmanifested consciousness or of unmanifested brahman. Pratyak turns the face inward toward the Self, against the outward bent that the senses impose by construction. Life-breath meeting inward awareness opens graded heights of lucidity, and the grade depends on mental purity, which depends on how the gates of sense are used. The Kaṭha lament agrees that the self-born Lord pierced the openings outward, so beings see outside, and only a rare wise soul reverses the gaze seeking deathlessness. To name Her inward awareness is to name the unmanifest ground in person. Contemplate: withdraw one sense deliberately and look back toward its seer.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-081"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-368", title: "Paśyantī", category: "Vāk-cit", summary: "She who is paśyantī, the second level of sound after parā in the svādhiṣṭāna chakra. Paśyantī is beholding speech, where the wedded stillness and power first show difference without division, sound grown perceptible yet still unuttered and unparted. The inward turn of the previous name flowers here into vision, the Self beginning to perceive all others as its own limbs. She presides over that dawning as both cradle and grave of utterance, from whom words rise and into whom they set. The seed has announced the tree without yet naming its fruit. Contemplate: catch the moment before speech when meaning already shines wordless.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-081"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-369", title: "Paradevatā", category: "Vāk-cit", summary: "She who is the supreme deity; parāśakti. Paradevatā states Her emperorship over every bright power: gods and goddesses spend the strength She transmits, lamps lit from one lamp. The name stands amid sound-doctrine like a seal, reminding that the supreme Word is also the supreme Person. Hierarchy exists to confess its summit. All altars secretly face one throne.  Even the loftiest thrones below Her borrow their luster, ruling only as regents of a power they cannot survey.  Liturgies therefore ascend the ranks only to lay their flowers at the highest footstool. Contemplate: honor your chosen deity as Her minister, and Her as their queen.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-081"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-370", title: "Madhyamā", category: "Vāk-cit", summary: "She who stays in the middle. Madhyamā is the inward, intermediate tide of speech between origin and utterance, where twoness first stirs though things remain unobjectified. Consciousness here is still impersonal while discernment begins to weigh and part, the inner instrument coloring awareness without yet delivering it to the world. This is the chamber of self-talk and of whispered sacred formulas, heard by the utterer alone, not even by one's own subtle envelope. Manifestation takes one more veiled step toward audibility.  Aspirants are taught to dwell there deliberately, repeating holy formulas where none but the soul can overhear. Contemplate: rehearse one sacred phrase mentally, letting only the inward ear attend.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-081"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-371", title: "Vaikharīrūpā", category: "Vāk-cit", summary: "She who is in the form of vaikharī (sound in the manifested, audible form). Vaikharī is speech delivered, shaped by transformed life-breath into fully materialized utterance dwelling in time and space, the non-supreme terminus of the scale. Whisper ripens into voice, and will itself is said to ground the passage until utterance merges back into awareness. The whole evolution turns on the handling of breath, matter's subtlest vehicle for spirit. What began as seed now stands as tree in flower and fruit.  Teachers therefore guard breath, diction and intention together, knowing that careless speech squanders gathered power. Contemplate: speak one true sentence slowly, feeling breath become meaning.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-081"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-372", title: "Bhaktamānasahaṃsikā", category: "Vāk-cit", summary: "She who is the swan in the minds of Her devotees. Haṃsikā recalls the creator's high lake of mind upon the snowy peak, whose fabled purity draws paired swans as the pure mind draws the wedded soul and ground. The lore of the great hymn adds the swan's genius: set before milk mixed with water, it drinks only milk, emblem of choosing the good from the mingled world. The pair further figure hearing and seeing, the two senses whose misuse most corrodes character, bidding devotees gather only what elevates. A later name will sing the same bird in sages' minds. Contemplate: practice the swan's art by extracting one good from today's mixture.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-081"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-373", title: "Kāmeśvaraprāṇanāḍī", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who is the very life of kāmeśvara, Her consort. Prāṇanāḍī confesses that the supreme still Lord breathes by Her, as the old Rudra-chant already invokes His healing gentleness inseparable from Her great auspicious form. The hymn to Her beauty remembers how poison from the milk-ocean's churning slept harmless in His throat by virtue of Her earring-grace, proving eternity itself leans on power to persist. Without Her moving tide, His stillness could neither save nor stir, and the worlds woven by Her glamour would lapse.  Devotion that grasps this dependence clings to Her first, understanding that honoring the source honors all its streams. Contemplate: thank Her for the very breath by which you think of Him.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-082"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-374", title: "Kṛtajñā", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who knows all of our actions as they occur. Kṛtajñā watches through nine witnesses no privacy escapes: sun, moon, the ender, time, and the five great elements, all Her officers. Yet knowing ripens into giving, for when workers act without clutching at fruit, pleased by their selflessness, She imparts supreme knowing in return. The chant-Upaniṣad reckons the five inward powers with the five environing ones as one winning throw, inward and environing together composing the radiant cosmic intellect, and calls their sum the good itself. She both audits the world and crowns its goodness. Contemplate: act once today as though nine gentle eyes approved.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-082"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-375", title: "Kāmapūjitā", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who is worshipped by kāma. Kāmapūjitā honors the love-god as one of the twelve royal votaries of Her fifteenfold mantra, serving Her by his own recension. Inwardly the name plants a flag at the first of four holy seats within the body, base, navel, heart and throat, answering the four tides of speech from supreme through beholding and mental to uttered. Sound's pilgrimage from root to lips is thus mapped as a round of shrines. Adoration ascends as utterance descends.  Lovers who worship love rightly find their own affection purified, turning possessive heat into selfless radiance. Contemplate: climb those four stations slowly with one mantra as your staff.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-082"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-376", title: "Śṛṅgārarasasampūrṇā", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who is filled with the essence of Love. Śṛṅgārarasa is the savor of love brimming over, tasted where the supreme Word reaches the navel seat, the second shrine, and the causal point becomes effective. Of the nine or ten savors catalogued, from heroism to wonder to peace, love is shown as fountainhead, and the great hymn arrays Her eightfold moods from battlefield valor to domestic warmth around this bridal core. Read by syllable-play the name also joins the primal veil with the full ground, the Real with and without attributes in one cup. The second holy seat is remembered here. Contemplate: let one affection today be consciously offered as worship.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-082"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-377", title: "Jayā", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who is victorious always and everywhere. Jayā is triumph as character rather than event, the habit of a wholeness that cannot be beaten. The inner reading bids winners of senses claim Her, since turbid organs corrupt the mind and bar realization, while trained ones leave serenity unruffled. The old Vaiṣṇava thousand likewise names the winner over all lives. Victory abroad follows victory within.  Inner triumphs precede outer ones, for the undefeated mind lends its courage to every enterprise of hand and tongue.  Even small contested efforts borrow that aura, turning routine persistence into quiet heroism. Contemplate: win one small mastery over impulse and dedicate the laurel to Her.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-082"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-378", title: "Jālandharasthitā", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who resides in the jālandhara pīṭha (in the throat region). Jālandhara names the heart-seat in this reckoning, third shrine of the inner round, where sound climbing from root and navel is refined into the mental tide before delivery. The tradition counts it among four bodily holy places answering the fourfold Word, and this name guards its penultimate ripening. What was beheld below becomes ponderable here.  Yogins locate devotional currents gathering there, the heart-lotus drinking sound as roots drink rain before flowering upward.  Attention held there refines emotion into devotion, as raw sound refines into intelligible prayer. Contemplate: hold a sacred phrase at the heart until it grows distinct.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-082"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-379", title: "Oḍyāṇapīṭhanilayā", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She whose abode is the center known as oḍyāṇa. Oḍyāṇa is the fourth seat, the throat wheel where fully ripened utterance issues as audible word. The pilgrimage that began as seed-throb at the base completes its ascent here, beholding turned to pondering turned to voice. To locate Her at the gate of delivery is to confess that every spoken truth exits through Her door.  Guardians of mantra therefore consecrate the voice before utterance, lest unclean speech profane so holy a doorway.  What passes that gate purely blesses hearers, carrying healing proportioned to its inward ripening. Contemplate: feel the throat as Her threshold before you speak.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-083"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-380", title: "Bindumaṇḍalavāsinī", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who resides in the bindumaṇḍala (in śrī chakra). Bindu is the central drop of the mystic diagram where She sits enthroned with Her Lord, reckoned its mightiest point because the pair abide there undivided. The same point answers the crown-orifice, the soft gate where cosmic current enters the frame alongside the hollow at the skull's base; bared to dawn and open sky these gates are said to drink health into the body. Geometry and physiology confess one sanctuary.  Pilgrims of the diagram journey inward petal by petal toward that point, leaving multiplicity behind with each nearer circuit. Contemplate: rest attention on the crown-point as on Her closed eyelid about to open.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-083"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-381", title: "Rahoyāgakramārādhyā", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who is worshipped in secret through sacrificial rites. Rahoyāga hides adoration from every eye but Her own, turning from outer fires and gestures to the subtlest figures enshrined within, above all the coiled power wedded to stillness at the crown. Outer performance scatters focus across implements, while inward offering holds the beam single until bliss supervenes and the practitioner grows happily bound to it. The schools divide the path accordingly into the interior observance and the exterior clan-rite, and the great hymn seats the blessed meditator in the nectar-ocean upon stillness as base and beneficence as cushion, two names for one non-dual couch. Contemplate: close the eyes and lay one inner flower where none can see.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-083"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-382", title: "Rahastarpaṇatarpitā", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who is to be gratified by the secret rites of worship. Rahastarpaṇa continues the hidden liturgy: mantras first steadied the straying mind, and each of the fifteen seeds repays study, but repetition must be wedded to the visualized form breathed alive by the meditative verse, until the deity Herself tutors by communion. Hence recitation is enjoined as purely mental. A deeper hearing offers all thirty-six principles, with their gathered twoness, as oblation into the bodily fire at the root, the interior lustration of the nine-enclosure rite in which dualities are burned away. Contemplate: pour one remembered duality quietly into that inner flame.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-083"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-383", title: "Sadyaḥprasādinī", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who bestows Her grace immediately. Sadyaḥprasādinī promises that the hidden road is also the short road: those who seek Her within, after the manner of the last two names, do not wait through ages for favor. Interiority removes the middlemen of delay, placing the seeker where grace already stands. Swiftness here measures nearness, not haste.  No ledger of merits delays the response, since nearness outruns earning and sincerity travels faster than ritual.  Seekers testify that a single sincere inward turning outweighs years of distracted outer performance. Contemplate: turn inward once with full sincerity and watch how quickly stillness answers.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-084"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-384", title: "Viśvasākṣiṇī", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who is witness to the whole universe. Viśvasākṣiṇī ascribes to Her the prerogative of the attributeless ground, to behold all doings while partaking in none. Every motion of the worlds passes before that gaze without staining it, as reflections cross a mirror. Witnessing is thus the highest actionless action.  Borrowing that gaze steadies judgment, letting events parade without dragging the watcher into their quarrel.  Such witness never wearies, never blinks and never accuses, holding all outcomes in level light. To rest in that impartial gaze is already a foretaste of freedom. Contemplate: borrow Her distance by watching one agitation without joining it.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-084"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-385", title: "Sākṣivarjitā", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who has no other witness. Sākṣivarjitā turns the gaze back on itself: the supreme witness is Herself unwitnessed, having no origin to be observed and no overseer to observe. Sourcelessness is not a lack but the signature of ultimacy. What sees all is seen by none.  Philosophy ends in reverent halt here, confessing that the ground of proof cannot itself be proven like an object.  Mystics therefore approach by negation and adoration together, unlearning grasp until only wonder remains. That reverent unknowing itself becomes the purest form of praise. Contemplate: seek the seer in yourself and notice it recede beyond every finding.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-084"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-386", title: "Ṣaḍaṅgadevatāyuktā", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who is accompanied by the deities of the six angās (heart, head, hair, eyes, armor and weapons). Ṣaḍaṅga recalls that every mantra marches with six limb-powers, honored on the practitioner's own frame by fingertip touches before and after recitation. Outward rite thus maps the body as temple furniture. Inwardly the six answer the six excellences of stillness, knowing all, completeness, supreme awareness, freedom, enduring might and endlessness, the marks of the attributeless ground. She stands ringed by them because She wears both faces, light and reflection.  Such attendance proves mantra alive, a retinue of powers escorting each sincere utterance toward its mark. Contemplate: touch heart, head and eyes with reverence before your next mantra.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-084"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-387", title: "Ṣāḍguṇyaparipūritā", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who is fully endowed with the six good qualities (prosperity, valor, dispassion, fame, wealth and wisdom). Ṣāḍguṇya arrays Her auspicious excellences: abundance, righteousness, renown, substance, discernment and the high aloofness that refuses favoritism beyond desert. The last virtue guards the rest, proving that impartiality belongs among perfections. She will not bend the moral order even for favorites. Glory here is principled, not arbitrary.  Seekers pray for shares of all six, prizing the last most, since unbiased grace crowns every other endowment.  Tradition counts impartiality the crown-jewel, since power without fairness would darken into mere force. Devotees beg that evenness above every glittering attainment. Contemplate: practice one even-handed kindness that profits you nothing.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-084"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-388", title: "Nityaklinnā", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who is ever compassionate. Nityaklinnā is mercy that never dries, the melting tenderness already sung as ocean, now shown as climate rather than weather. The lunar round keeps the memory, giving the third night's attendant the same melting name. Constancy outranks intensity in Her court.  Seasons cannot stale that tenderness, nor familiarity tire it, for its fountain refills faster than hearts can draw. Nightly remembrance of this melting name softens even disciplined minds toward surrender and trust.  Litanies place Her melting tenderness beside cosmic majesty, lest awe forget approachability. Devotees lean on that softness whenever discipline grows dry and the heart seeks shelter. Contemplate: let one habitual response grow permanently gentler.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-085"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-389", title: "Nirupamā", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who is incomparable. Nirupamā refuses every parallel, since the finite cannot meter the infinite, as the white-ash teaching says there is no describing Him beyond saying He is what He is. The great commentator adds that He is one without second, limitless, knowable only through His very uniqueness, which is another way of keeping silence. The tale is told of two brothers examined by their father: the elder lectured at length while the younger said nothing, and the father judged the silent one the knower, since speech about the ground errs by finishing it. Contemplate: answer one mystery today with reverent silence.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-085"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-390", title: "Nirvāṇa sukhadāyinī", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who confers the bliss of Liberation. Nirvāṇa unties the body-knot: freed from flesh, mind wakes to happiness, and when bodily afflictions dissolve, timeless joy remains for those who sought Her in secret. The Gītā-voice pictures the inward-glad seer resting on the inner ground, alight with inner light, sins burnt and doubts cut, laboring for all beings while abiding in the ground. Bliss is therefore not prize but nature recovered.  Rites, vigils and secret offerings all aim at this unknotting, when the freed spark rests luminous in its source. Contemplate: loosen one bodily tension as a rehearsal for that final unknotting.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-085"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-391", title: "Nityā-ṣoḍaśikārūpā", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who is in the form of the sixteen daily deities (i.e., kāmeśvari, bhagamālinī, nityaklinnā, bheruṇḍā, vahnivāsinī, mahāvajreśvarī, śivadūtī, tvaritā, kulasundarī, nityā, nīlapatākinī, vijayā, sarvamangalā, jvālāmālinī, citrā and tripurasundarī). Nityāṣoḍaśikā gathers the fifteen lunar attendants honored in the mystic diagram, answering the fifteen mantra-seeds, with the Mother Herself as sixteenth answering the sixteenfold mantra crowned by fortune's seed, the mightiest formula reserved for final release. The name also recalls the solemn five-day fire-rite with its sixteen priests, judging one heartfelt recitation weightier than a thousand such performances. In the dark fortnight She is said to rest in the solar orb, in the bright in the lunar, dividing day-practice of the fifteenfold from night-practice of the sixteenfold. Contemplate: greet the moon's phases as Her own sixteenfold smile.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-085"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-392", title: "Śrīkaṇṭhārdhaśarīriṇī", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who possesses half of the body of śrīkaṇṭha (shiva). She who is in the form of ardhanāriśvara. Śrīkaṇṭha is the fair-throated Lord who harbors poison at His neck, and She occupies His left half as the half-male half-female Lord, husband and wife sharing one frame. The forest-teaching traces the pattern to the prime person swelling into embrace and splitting into two halves like a divided pea, wife completing husband into fruitfulness. Letters mystically agree, for the primal vowel, supreme Word ripening into utterance, counts as half of all speech put together. Anatomy, wedlock and alphabet confess one halving. Contemplate: honor the masculine and feminine halves of your own nature as one body.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-085"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-393", title: "Prabhāvatī", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who is effulgent. Prabhāvatī shines ringed by eight bright attendants embodying the classic attainments, from minuteness and lightness to lordship, sway, wish-fulfilment, arrival and every desire, each a ray from Her feet. The great hymn marvels that votaries of that foot-radiance win salutation from the worlds. Glory here is not solitary blaze but a court of kindled powers.  Votaries circle that radiance in meditation, borrowing brilliance until their own faces shine with borrowed dawn.  Their faces are said to brighten after such circling, carrying temple-light into marketplace and home. Contemplate: picture eight small lamps lit from the single lamp of Her feet.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-086"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-394", title: "Prabhārūpā", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who is effulgence. Prabhārūpā advances from bearer to substance: the radiance attending Her in the last name is Her very stuff, supreme light underived. The chant-teaching glimpses Him as mind-led, subtle-bodied and luminous, the inward splendor to which all outer lamps defer. Where the previous name showed the court, this one shows the queen as shining itself.  Mystics close the eyes to see it better, finding interior noon outshining every sunrise they have known.  Such brightness asks no fuel and casts no shadow, self-subsisting splendor beyond all contrast of day and night. Contemplate: meditate not on a bright object but on brightness as such.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-086"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-395", title: "Prasiddhā", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who is celebrated. Prasiddhā is known to all because She is the all-knower turned inward: as the inner Self of every breathing thing, whenever anyone says I, it is Her stirring at the root of the pronoun. Fame here is not reputation but self-evidence, the impossibility of overlooking oneself. The most famous thing is the closest.  Pilgrims, scholars and children alike confess it untaught, since selfhood needs no introduction to itself.  No herald need announce such presence, for breathing itself testifies unbrokenly to the dweller within. Even denial borrows Her light, since the denier must exist to deny. Contemplate: hear your own I as Her footnote.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-086"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-396", title: "Parameśvarī", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who is the supreme sovereign. Parameśvarī rules doubly, by Her own absoluteness and as bride of the supreme Lord, administrator able and unquestioned. The Vaiṣṇava thousand honors the same station under the masculine ending. The Gītā-voice defines true sight as perceiving that imperishable overlord dwelling equally in all perishing creatures. Supremacy thus proves itself by impartial indwelling rather than distant command.  True rulers study that pattern, learning that authority deepens precisely where it indwells and serves impartially.  Communities flourish where that pattern rules, since shared reverence steadies law better than fear. Contemplate: bow inwardly to the same sovereign in a stranger.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-086"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-397", title: "Mūlaprakṛtiḥ", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who is the first cause of the entire universe. Mūlaprakṛti is root nature, the veiling yet mothering power on whom the still ground depends to create and keep. She carries the three strands in poise with the three creative tides of willing, knowing and doing; when their balance tips, the passive soul is clothed and its sleeping momentum unfolds. The lore of beginnings traces space, air, fire, water and earth issuing in train, then plants, beasts and humans, from the existent that resolved to be many. Unmanifest poise precedes all this, and beyond poise the attributeless alone abides. Contemplate: trace your own restlessness back to some tipped balance and offer it to the root.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-086"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-398", title: "Avyaktā", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who is unmanifested. Avyaktā extends the root into its stillest poise, nature with the three strands perfectly equipoised, the ground's first and parentless stage, also called the fourth state beyond waking, dreaming and deep sleep, where non-twoneess is realized. The aphorism of the threads declares that ultimate unmanifest, never caught by eye, speech, austerity or deed, imperceptible because never objectified. When the attributeless stirs toward making, the veil takes its first modification, and manifestation begins.  Behind every formed thing that poise abides untouched, the unpictured canvas on which all pictures are painted. Contemplate: settle attention where nothing yet appears and honor that as Her oldest face.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-086"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-399", title: "Vyaktāvyaktasvarūpīṇi", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who is in the manifested and unmanifested forms. Vyaktāvyakta holds both banks of becoming: the unmanifest imperishable and the manifest perishable meet in Her single person. This first of manifested modes is called the great one, the cosmic intelligence rich with supreme knowing, undifferentiated nature turning toward expression, from which all further unfolding descends. The soul-ground kinship is sealed here, and the stanza quietly claims both ends of hope, creation's dawn and final release, as Her doing.  Philosophers name that junction the great turning, where the formless first consents to wear a face for love of the world. Contemplate: greet one ending and one beginning today as Her two hands.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-086"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-400", title: "Vyāpinī", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who is all-pervading. Vyāpinī follows necessarily from roothood and unmanifest breadth: as first cause and as the unconditioned expanse, She saturates whatever is, leaving no outside. Elsewhere She is called the One for the same reason, unity proved by ubiquity. Pervasion is not extension through space but the ground's manner of being everywhere without moving.  Wherever beings wander, that presence has arrived beforehand, holding each place open like sky holds birds.  Saints report meeting Her in market, forest and sickroom alike, since no itinerary outruns omnipresence. Wherever attention settles sincerely, that ground already waits beneath it. Contemplate: pause between two breaths and feel the Pervader already there.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-087"] },
];
